//! std.http — an HTTP client, plus the `Request`/`Response`/`HttpError` types the
//! server shares.
//!
//! Import as: import std.http
//!
//! ```thera
//! let resp = http.get('https://example.com/api/thing')?;
//! if resp.is_ok() {
//!     let doc = resp.json()?;
//! }
//!
//! let made = http.post('https://example.com/things', body: payload.bytes(),
//!     headers: ['content-type': 'application/json'])?;
//! ```
//!
//! **`http://` and `https://`.** An `https` URL verifies the server's certificate
//! chain and host name against the bundled roots; a certificate that doesn't check
//! out is an `HttpError.Tls` — its own variant because, unlike a `Connect`,
//! retrying will not fix it. A 4xx/5xx is a `Response`, not an error: `HttpError`
//! is for exchanges that did not happen. Redirects are not followed, and there is no
//! connection pooling (one connection per request).
//!
//! `get`/`post`/`send` buffer the body, which is what almost every caller wants.
//! For one that should be consumed as it arrives — a `text/event-stream`, a large
//! download — `stream` returns once the head is in, with the body as an
//! `io.Reader`; `with_stream` is the same thing with the close taken care of:
//!
//! ```thera
//! http.with_stream(request, handle: (s) => {
//!     for ev in sse.events(s.body) { … }   // std.http.sse
//!     return Result.Ok(void);
//! })?;
//! ```
//!
//! This is a barrel: it re-exports its sibling files (see docs/language.md) and
//! defines nothing of its own. The split is deliberate — `std.http.server` imports
//! the `wire` sibling **directly**, so importing the server never pulls in the
//! client, nor (once it lands) TLS. `wire` is the shared third library; it just
//! isn't a third public name, because the namespace is the last dotted segment and
//! `http.get(url)` should stay the spelling of the most-written line here.
//!
//! Serving requests is `std.http.server`, a separate import — see docs/stdlib.md
//! § `std.http`. Decoding a `text/event-stream` is `std.http.sse`, likewise
//! separate: it needs nothing from here (any `io.Reader` will do), and keeping it
//! out of this namespace keeps `Event` and `Decoder` from becoming `http.Event` and
//! `http.Decoder`.

pub import 'wire'; // Request, Response, HttpError, status codes, the HTTP/1.1 codec
pub import 'client'; // get / post / send
